A||||3||DTH||rear wheel bearings||||5||
Z||000000||DTH||10-02-2001||09:14 PM||||I was wondering does anybody know how often the rear wheel bearings should be greased? In the Haynes manual it says not to use the old hub nut again. Is there a reason for this?  Thanks ahead of time.     Dan ||||reg||5
Z||000001||wantav8||10-02-2001||09:17 PM||||You can not grease the rear wheel bearings. They are sealed units. The nut is self locking and needs replaced to retain security. A new one comes with the rear hub. I paid 65.00 for mine. Took about an hour to replace at 122,000 miles<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/wantav8.jpg"> <p>[This message has been edited by wantav8 (edited 10-03-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||LivoniaFieroGT||10-02-2001||09:58 PM||||On all GM cars the "locking" hub nut is never to be used.  It is thought to possibly lose its integrity if torqued on more than the one time. It is advisable (not mentioned in manuals - but we did this at GM) to use the old nut to reseat the axle. Then remove and install the new nut.  That way you are not using the new nut to torque the axle into place and possible get a false reading.<BR>Rob||||reg||4
Z||000003||theogre||10-02-2001||11:48 PM||||I don't recomend reusing one unless you absolutly have to. Torque specs for new/used nuts are in my cave and Haynes book.<P>removing/installing the nut heats it. allot. hot enough to burn you at times. That screws with the tempering of the nut. It also wears quite a bit. It could strip or cross thread if it's reused too many times. Since you likely can't be sure who lese may have had it off.... I wouldn't trust them.<P>Nut may or maynot come with new hubs/axles. Depends where you get them. But don't worry... MotorMite has them available in their product lines with along many other axle/spindle nuts. they aren't hard to get. Cost a couple dollars.<P>In Fiero, the nut is a little less critical than a FWD car. The axle can't come out even with the nut removed. I've tried it... (It can come out durring cornering on some FWD.) It can however come loose, make noise, and cause wear in the splines or posibly bind the inner CV joint if the nut backs off more than a few turns. Neither is good. spend the few dollars for new ones. It's cheap insurance against problems.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||4
